# ─── Interrelated environment ────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Copy to .env and fill in real values. .env is gitignored; this file is the
# single documented registry of every variable the system reads. If a module
# reads an env var that is not documented here, that is a bug in the module.
#
# LINE ENDINGS MATTER: this repo forces LF via .gitattributes because a CRLF
# ending inside .env once corrupted a Postgres password on a sibling project
# (the password became "value\r" inside the Linux container). If you edit
# .env by hand on Windows, keep the file LF.

# ─── Postgres ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Local dev + tests: the docker-compose database service reads these to
# provision itself, and the realtime service reads them to connect. In
# production these point at Neon, and POSTGRES_SSL must be "true".
POSTGRES_HOST=localhost
# The HOST-side published port for the compose database (containers always
# talk to database:5432 internally). If you run a native Postgres on this
# machine it already owns 5432 — connections would silently land on the
# wrong server and fail auth — so pick a free port like 5433 here.
POSTGRES_PORT=5433
POSTGRES_USER=interrelated
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=change-me-please
POSTGRES_DB=interrelated
POSTGRES_SSL=false

# ─── Realtime service ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
BACKEND_PORT=3000

# ─── Ingest worker ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# "1" starts the in-process ingest worker at boot (server.ts). Opt-in
# because the worker POLLS Postgres — free against a local database, but on
# Neon it would hold compute awake around the clock, which is why render.yaml
# pins it to "0" until M3. Both compose stacks set it to "1" themselves; set
# it here only when running `npm run dev` outside compose.
INGEST_WORKER=0
# Queue poll interval in ms (default 5000). Only read when the worker runs.
#INGEST_POLL_MS=5000
# Embedding provider for ingested chunks until per-org BYO keys land in M3:
#   mock  — deterministic fake vectors; zero downloads; what tests/CI use.
#   local — fastembed/ONNX bge-small-en-v1.5; ~30 MB model download on first
#           use, ~300 MB RAM while embedding. Host-side `npm run dev` only
#           (fastembed lives in the ROOT package's devDependencies — the
#           compose image ships without it), and never Render (512 MB).
EMBEDDING_PROVIDER=mock

# ─── LLM provider (server boot + ask CLI; per-org BYO keys arrive in M3) ─────
# Which generation provider the widget chat route and `npm run ask` use.
# "mock" (the default) answers by quoting the retrieved context — the full
# grounded loop with zero keys, which is what compose stacks and CI run.
# Server-level env config is a stopgap M3 replaces with per-org encrypted
# credentials; it never serves real tenants.
#   groq   — needs GROQ_API_KEY (free tier, no card: console.groq.com)
#   gemini — needs GEMINI_API_KEY (free tier, no card: aistudio.google.com)
#   ollama — needs a local Ollama and OLLAMA_MODEL (a model you have pulled)
LLM_PROVIDER=mock
#GROQ_API_KEY=
#GROQ_MODEL=llama-3.3-70b-versatile
#GEMINI_API_KEY=
#GEMINI_MODEL=gemini-2.5-flash
#OLLAMA_BASE_URL=http://localhost:11434
#OLLAMA_MODEL=llama3.2

# ─── Widget surface (M2.5) ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
# HMAC secret for widget session tokens, 32+ chars. Unset/empty → an
# ephemeral random secret per boot: fine in dev (the widget re-mints after
# a restart), wrong in production (every deploy logs visitors out) — Render
# prompts for it via render.yaml.
#WIDGET_TOKEN_SECRET=
# Per-org answers per UTC day, checked BEFORE the model call. Flat default
# (200) until per-plan caps arrive with M5 billing.
#WIDGET_DAILY_ANSWER_CAP=200
# Groundedness gate threshold override (max dense cosine distance). The
# 0.34 default was derived from the eval set for bge-small-en-v1.5
# (npm run eval -- --sweep-threshold; analysis in eval/RESULTS.md) — a
# different embedding model needs its own sweep before overriding here.
#ANSWER_MAX_DISTANCE=0.34

# Publishable key for the /demo page (routes/demo.ts). Not a secret — pk
# values are public by design. Unset → /demo serves setup instructions.
# Printed by: npm run seed-demo -- --corpus fastify --origin <origin>
#DEMO_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=

# ─── Dashboard (web/) — auth at rest (M3.2) ──────────────────────────────────
# web/ reads the same POSTGRES_* variables above: in dev its next.config.ts
# loads THIS file (already-set env always wins); on Vercel they are project
# env — point POSTGRES_HOST at Neon's POOLED "-pooler" host there, because
# serverless instances multiply client pools.
#
# Both secrets below are UNSET in dev on purpose: the code falls back to
# published development values with a loud warning, and production REFUSES
# TO BOOT without real ones (web/src/instrumentation.ts).
#
# Blind-index pepper for email lookups (web/src/lib/auth/emailCrypto.ts).
# Any long random string. Changing it orphans every account — lookups stop
# matching — so treat it like a key, not like config.
#EMAIL_INDEX_PEPPER=
# AES-256-GCM key for email-at-rest, exactly 32 bytes of base64. Generate:
#   node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('base64'))"
#EMAIL_ENCRYPTION_KEY=
# "1" skips the Have-I-Been-Pwned breached-password screen at signup —
# for offline dev and tests only (the check already fails OPEN on network
# trouble, so leaving it on costs nothing when HIBP is reachable).
#BREACH_CHECK_DISABLED=0

# ─── Internal API — dashboard ↔ realtime (M3.4) ──────────────────────────────
# The server-to-server surface web/ proxies provider-credential operations
# through. ALL-OR-NOTHING PAIR on the realtime side: the secret without the
# master key refuses to boot (server.ts) — it would accept tenant keys it
# cannot encrypt. Unset → the /internal/* routes do not exist (404).
#
# Shared secret, 32+ chars, set IDENTICALLY here (realtime reads it) and in
# web's environment (the client sends it as x-internal-secret).
#INTERNAL_API_SECRET=
# AES-256-GCM master key for tenant provider keys at rest, 32 bytes base64.
# realtime-only — web never holds it, so a web compromise cannot decrypt
# the credential table. NO dev fallback, unlike the email keys: a provider
# key is real even in dev. Generate:
#   node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('base64'))"
#CREDENTIAL_MASTER_KEY=
# Where web finds realtime's internal API. Dev: realtime's npm run dev /
# compose port. Vercel: the Render service URL.
#REALTIME_INTERNAL_URL=http://localhost:3000
